      <h2 id="how" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How AAIP Draws Work</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">The Worker EOI and its score</h3>
      <p>
        To be considered for any AAIP worker stream you must submit a Worker Expression of Interest (WEOI) in the AAIP portal. You self-declare your details, and the system scores you on a points grid with a <strong>maximum of 100 points</strong>: up to 69 for human capital (education, language, work experience, age and family in Alberta) and up to 31 for economic factors tied to your Alberta job offer. There is no minimum score to submit. Since April 7, 2026 the WEOI costs $135, and it stays in the pool for one year from the date the fee was paid. Your score is visible under "Check Existing WEOI" in the portal.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Separate draws for each stream, pathway and sector</h3>
      <p>
        Alberta selects from the pool through targeted draws. In 2026 these covered the Alberta Opportunity Stream, the Rural Renewal Stream, the Tourism and Hospitality Stream, the Dedicated Health Care Pathways (with and without Express Entry), and the Alberta Express Entry Stream, which itself has the Accelerated Tech Pathway, the Law Enforcement Pathway and priority sector draws for health care, construction, manufacturing, agriculture, aviation and skilled trades. Alberta says its 2026 priorities are health care, technology, construction, manufacturing, aviation, agriculture and Rural Renewal communities, though not exclusively.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Why the minimum score differs by pathway</h3>
      <p>
        Because each draw only considers candidates who fit it, you compete with a different group in each one. Three things push the cut-off up or down:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Supply and demand.</strong> The Tourism and Hospitality Stream had 2,846 EOIs in the pool for a 2026 allocation of only 156 nominations, so its draws had the highest minimum scores of the year (71 to 73). The Law Enforcement pool had 39 EOIs, and its draws went as low as 46.</li>
        <li><strong>Factors that earn no points.</strong> The WEOI also collects your immigration status, your Express Entry profile, the NOC code of your job offer, your employer's NAICS industry code, and your wage and hours. None of these add points, but AAIP uses them to target sector draws. A health care or aviation draw can therefore invite someone with a lower score than a general draw.</li>
        <li><strong>Score is not the only factor.</strong> Alberta states that it considers a variety of factors in Worker EOIs and, to protect program integrity, does not disclose recent draw parameters. Matching a past minimum score is not a guarantee of an invitation.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Alberta can also invite you to a stream other than the one you picked in your WEOI. If you are weighing Alberta against B.C., our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-alberta-aaip-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP vs Alberta AAIP comparison</a> sets the two systems side by side, and the <a href="/articles/alberta-pnp-guide" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Alberta PNP guide</a> explains each stream's eligibility rules.
      </p>

      <h2 id="trends" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What the 2026 Alberta PNP Draw Numbers Show</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Alberta Opportunity Stream draws have shrunk</h3>
      <p>
        From February to August, Alberta Opportunity Stream draws were large: 915 invitations on February 2, 832 on March 5 and May 6, 993 on May 27, 833 on July 15 and 743 on August 14. After 575 on September 1, the next three draws invited only 113, 123 and 132 candidates. The processing page shows why the stream has little room left: of its 3,562 nominations for 2026, 3,386 had been issued by November 4, leaving 176. Minimum scores in these smaller draws (54 to 59) sat in the middle of the year's 50 to 65 range.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Express Entry priority sectors keep going past their allocation</h3>
      <p>
        Priority sector draws under the Alberta Express Entry Stream have already issued 703 nominations against a 2026 allocation of 619. Alberta notes that Express Entry allocations may be exceeded under a small administrative overage from the federal government. The two largest recent draws were "General" priority sector rounds: 200 invitations at 64 on September 29 and 386 at 60 on October 20. The Accelerated Tech Pathway had 23 spaces left out of 619.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Rural Renewal and health care stay accessible</h3>
      <p>
        Rural Renewal Stream draws were the most stable all year, with minimum scores between 50 and 55; every one since August 11 was at 51. The non-Express Entry Dedicated Health Care Pathway produced the lowest score of 2026, 45, on January 27 and June 9. Physicians and Francophones nominated under extra federal spaces don't count toward Alberta's allocation; 50 physicians and 12 Francophones had been nominated this way by November 4.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Invitations peaked in spring</h3>
      <p>
        Counting numbered invitations by month, May was the busiest (2,590), followed by February (2,285) and June (1,823). October had 958. With only 495 nominations left for the year, a slower pace is consistent with a program running out of 2026 spaces, but Alberta has not said how many draws remain.
      </p>

      <h2 id="next" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">When Will the Next Alberta PNP Draw Be?</h2>
      <p>
        Alberta does not publish a schedule. Its own words: "AAIP draws are not regularly scheduled. They are done as needed to address sector needs and provincial priorities." We won't guess a date. What the record does show is that, from late January to early November 2026, Alberta never went more than 14 days without a draw, and draws for different streams often came only days apart. The longest gaps were July 21 to August 4 (14 days), August 19 to September 1 (13 days) and March 27 to April 8 (12 days). The best approach is practical: keep your WEOI accurate and current, check the processing information page and your email, and have your documents ready so you can act within the 15-day invitation window. Alberta had not published 2027 allocation figures on its processing page as of November 12, 2026.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Key Takeaway</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">Compare your score only with draws in the stream or sector that fits your job offer. A 58 is below every 2026 Tourism and Hospitality cut-off but above every Rural Renewal cut-off. For the B.C. equivalent, see our <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="underline font-semibold">BC PNP draw tracker</a>.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="improve" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Improve Your Alberta Worker EOI Score</h2>
      <p>
        The points grid (version dated September 28, 2026) rewards a handful of things you can sometimes change. Claim only what you can prove: AAIP checks your WEOI against your application, and inaccurate information can lead to refusal.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Factor</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Maximum points</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">How to gain points</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Language</td><td class="px-4 py-3">10 + 3 bilingual</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 6 or higher in English earns 10 (NCLC 6 in French earns 8), scored on your lowest skill. CLB/NCLC 4 in both languages adds 3.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Education</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12 + 10</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Doctorate 12, master's 10, bachelor's or trades certificate 7. Completing it in Alberta adds 10, elsewhere in Canada 6.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Work experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">11 + 10</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12+ months total earns 11. Six months or more in Alberta adds 10, or 6 in another province.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Age</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5</td><td class="px-4 py-3">21 to 34 earns 5; 35 to 49 earns 4.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Family in Alberta</td><td class="px-4 py-3">8</td><td class="px-4 py-3">A parent, child or sibling in Alberta who is a citizen or PR over 18.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Alberta job offer</td><td class="px-4 py-3">10 + 6</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Permanent full-time offer earns 10. A Rural Renewal endorsement, an eligible tourism and hospitality employer, or a police employer in the AACP adds 6.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Job location</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Any job outside the Calgary and Edmonton census metropolitan areas.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Regulated occupation</td><td class="px-4 py-3">10</td><td class="px-4 py-3">An Alberta licence or certification for your regulated job, such as an Alberta Qualification Certificate or an AIT-recognized trade certificate.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The quickest gains are usually language and licensing. Retaking a test to move your weakest skill from CLB 5 to CLB 6 adds 2 points, and from CLB 4 to CLB 6 adds 5. Getting Alberta certification in a regulated trade or profession is worth 10. A job in a smaller Alberta community adds 5 location points, and with a Rural Renewal endorsement another 6, which helps explain why Rural Renewal draws work for candidates with modest human capital scores. To update a submitted WEOI, use "Edit" in the portal. While you are editing, your WEOI is not eligible for selection, and if you don't save within an hour it reverts unchanged. Editing does not extend its one-year validity.
      </p>

      <h2 id="after" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Happens After an Alberta PNP Invitation</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Check eligibility first.</strong> Your portal status changes to "Selected." Read the rules of the stream you were invited to and the Worker Stream Document Checklist. If you don't qualify, decline: refused applications lose both fees.</li>
        <li><strong>Create the application within 15 days.</strong> Click the link and save the first page to create a draft. If you do nothing, the WEOI returns to the pool.</li>
        <li><strong>Submit within 30 days and pay $1,500.</strong> The worker stream application fee must be paid within 24 hours of submission or the application is cancelled. You leave the WEOI pool while it is in progress.</li>
        <li><strong>Wait for assessment.</strong> Alberta lists processing by cut-off date rather than weeks. On November 4, 2026 it was assessing Alberta Opportunity Stream applications received on or before August 2, 2026, Rural Renewal applications up to October 9, Dedicated Health Care applications up to September 25 and Accelerated Tech applications up to July 8.</li>
        <li><strong>Apply for permanent residence.</strong> If nominated, you apply to IRCC through Express Entry or a paper-based application, depending on your stream.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Comparing provinces? Estimate where you would stand in British Columbia with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a>, then check recent B.C. cut-offs on the <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP draws page</a>.
      </p>

